# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BULK SMS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A coordinator can text every member registered in their territory from
# /admin/broadcast. The recipient list is built on the server from their seat,
# so there is nothing here that widens it — these variables only decide which
# gateway carries the message.
#
# Leave SMS_PROVIDER unset and the page still works: the count, the cost and
# the recipient list are all live, and the send button refuses with a message
# naming the variable that is missing. It never pretends to have sent.
#
#   SMS_PROVIDER    termii | africastalking | console
#   SMS_SENDER_ID   The alphanumeric sender the networks display, max 11
#                   characters, e.g. MAP. In Nigeria this must be registered
#                   with the gateway BEFORE it will deliver — an unregistered
#                   sender ID is the usual reason a test send reports success
#                   and no phone rings.
#
# console: nothing leaves the server. Every message is written to the log and
# the broadcast is recorded with provider "console" so the history table shows
# what really happened. Use it to walk a coordinator through the screen.

SMS_PROVIDER=console
SMS_SENDER_ID=MAP

# ── Termii ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The usual choice for Nigerian bulk SMS. Key from the dashboard at termii.com.
# TERMII_CHANNEL is "generic" for promotional traffic and "dnd" to reach
# numbers on the Do-Not-Disturb list, which is most Nigerian mobiles — a
# congress notice that never arrives is nearly always this setting.

# TERMII_API_KEY=
# TERMII_CHANNEL=dnd
# TERMII_BASE_URL=https://api.ng.termii.com

# ── Africa's Talking ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The second string. Username is "sandbox" on their test account.

# AT_USERNAME=
# AT_API_KEY=

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ERROR REPORTING
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# With nothing set, every unhandled error is written as one structured JSON
# line that any hosting platform's log viewer can search and alert on. That is
# the floor, and it costs nothing.
#
# Set SENTRY_DSN and the same errors are also posted to Sentry. lib/report.js
# speaks the store endpoint directly rather than pulling in the SDK — if you
# later want tracing and session replay, install @sentry/nextjs and delete that
# one function; every caller already goes through report().

# SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<org>.ingest.sentry.io/<project>

# ── Two-factor authentication ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing to configure. TOTP is RFC 6238 over node:crypto (lib/totp.js) and
# needs no service and no key of its own — the per-account secret is generated
# at enrolment and the recovery codes are hashed with SESSION_SECRET.
#
# §13.2 makes it mandatory at state level and above. That is enforced as a wall
# in front of /admin, not as a refusal at sign-in: a coordinator locked out of
# everything on election morning is a worse outcome than the risk, so they can
# still sign in, still reach /portal, and see nothing in the secretariat but
# the enrolment screen until it is on.
#
# Any member may turn it on for themselves at /admin/security.
#
# NOTE FOR THE DEMO LOGINS ABOVE: the top three tiers — National Coordinator,
# Assistant Coordinator, Zonal Coordinator and State Coordinator — are redirected
# to /secure on their first sign-in and asked to enrol before the secretariat
# opens. That is not a bug and it is not a setting to switch off; it is the
# control working. Scan the QR with any authenticator app, enter the six digits,
# and write down the ten recovery codes it gives you. LGA and Ward Coordinators,
# Polling Unit Coordinators and ordinary members are not asked.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# RATE LIMITING
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# With nothing set, the counters live in the app's own memory. That stops a
# script trying ten thousand passwords against one number from a laptop, which
# is the attack this platform will actually see — but on a plan that runs
# several instances, an attacker gets one window per instance.
#
# Set these two and every instance shares one counter in Redis. Upstash is
# spoken over its REST API rather than through a client library: one fetch, no
# connection pool for a serverless instance to own, and no dependency.
#
# If Redis is unreachable the app falls back to the in-process counter rather
# than refusing requests. A rate limiter that takes the site down when its own
# dependency blinks is a worse outage than the attack it prevents.

# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://<name>.upstash.io
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
